/-
`SU(3, 2^n)` is perfect and `PSU(3, 2^n)` is perfect, for `n ≥ 2`.

The argument is the classical one for a rank-one group of Lie type of characteristic two.
Write `G = SU(3,q)`, `q = 2^n`, with Borel `B = U ⋊ T`, Weyl element `w = J3` and
Bruhat decomposition `G = B ∪ B w B`.

* `U` is generated by commutators `[t, u]` with `t ∈ T`, `u ∈ U`: for suitable `t` (a prime
  element of `Fˣ`) the map `u ↦ [t,u]` is injective on the finite group `U`, hence bijective.
  Therefore `U ⊆ G'`; by normality also `U⁻ = w U w ⊆ G'`.
* `w = u(0,1) · v(0,1) · u(0,1)` where `v(b,c) = w u(b,c) w`, so `w ∈ ⟨U, U⁻⟩`.
* For `a ∈ Fˣ` and `b ∈ Fˣ` with `b^(q+1) = a + a^q` the identity
  `t(a) = u(b,a) · v(b/a, a^(-q)) · u(b a^(q-1), a) · w` holds; in particular, choosing `a`
  of order `q²-1` and `b` with `b^(q+1) = a+a^q`, the whole torus `T = ⟨t(a)⟩` lies in
  `⟨U, U⁻⟩`.
* Hence `G = B ∪ BwB ⊆ ⟨U, U⁻⟩ ⊆ G'`, i.e. `G` is perfect.
-/
